{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails fundamental accessibility requirements at the structural level. It uses a non-interactive `<div>` for a toggle trigger, completely omits keyboard event handling, lacks all required ARIA states/properties for the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern, and exposes decorative symbols to assistive technology without hiding them. This is not an 80% implementation; it is a 0% accessible disclosure widget that blocks keyboard users entirely and provides zero programmatic context to screen readers.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading the code, I predicted: (1) `<div>` masking native button semantics, (2) missing `aria-expanded` state synchronization, (3) absent keyboard event handlers (`onKeyDown`), (4) unhidden decorative `+`/`-` symbols, and (5) missing `aria-controls` association. All predictions were confirmed during investigation. No surprises; the implementation is structurally incomplete.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Non-interactive `<div>` used as clickable trigger**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11` where a `<div>` with only `onClick` replaces a native `<button>`. Keyboard users cannot reach or activate this element via Tab/Enter/Space. Screen readers do not announce it as interactive. Per WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) and 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), interactive elements must be natively focusable and operable. Fix: Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\">`.\n\n2. **Missing `aria-expanded` state synchronization**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11-14`. The trigger toggles visual content but has no ARIA attribute reflecting `isExpanded`. Screen reader users hear static text and have no programmatic way to know the current expanded/collapsed state. Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the trigger must expose `aria-expanded={isExpanded}`. Fix: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the button element.\n\n3. **Missing `aria-controls` association & content panel ID**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11` (trigger) and `:16` (content). There is no programmatic link between the trigger and the panel it controls. Screen reader users cannot navigate directly to the controlled region or understand the relationship. Per WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) and WAI-ARIA APG, the trigger must reference the panel's ID via `aria-controls`. Fix: Add `id=\"expandable-content-{title}\"` to the content `<div>` and `aria-controls={...}` to the button.\n\n4. **Decorative `+`/`-` symbols exposed to assistive technology**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:13`. The symbols are rendered as plain text, causing screen readers to announce \"plus\" or \"minus\" alongside the title. This creates redundant/confusing announcements when `aria-expanded` is added. Per WCAG 4.1.2 and known anti-patterns for state indicators, decorative symbols must be hidden from AT when state is communicated programmatically. Fix: Wrap in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\"> {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'} </span>` or replace with screen-reader-friendly text/icons (e.g., `aria-label={isExpanded ? 'Collapse section' : 'Expand section'}`).\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing keyboard event handlers**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11`. Even if `tabIndex={0}` were added to the `<div>`, Enter/Space would not trigger `toggle` because only `onClick` is attached. Per WCAG 2.1.1, interactive elements must support standard keyboard activation. Fix: Add `onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.preventDefault(); toggle(); } }}` to the button.\n\n2. **Missing focus indicator styling**. Not visible in the provided code. Without explicit `:focus-visible` or `:focus` CSS, keyboard users will lack a visible focus ring on the trigger. Per WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible), focus indicators must be clearly visible with minimum 3:1 contrast change against adjacent colors. Fix: Add `.expandable-trigger:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 2px; }`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Missing heading semantics for the title**. See `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12`. The title is rendered as plain text inside a button, breaking information architecture. Screen readers rely on heading hierarchy to navigate content structure. Fix: If this component represents a section header, consider wrapping it in `<h3>` (or appropriate level) outside the button, or use `aria-label` on the button if the title is purely functional.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Replace `+`/`-` symbols with explicit text like \"Expand\" / \"Collapse\" for better cognitive clarity and language independence.\n- Add `aria-describedby` to the trigger pointing to a visually hidden instruction if complex behavior exists (not required here, but good practice for custom widgets).\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Native HTML replacement**: ARIA is entirely absent because the component uses `<div>` instead of `<button>`. Native semantics must come first.\n- **Focus management on state change**: When `isExpanded` becomes true, focus remains on the trigger (acceptable for disclosure widgets), but there is no mechanism to move focus into the newly revealed content if it contains interactive elements. This should be documented or handled explicitly.\n- **Visual-only state indicators**: The `+`/`-` relies purely on visual position/symbol. If CSS fails or colors change, state becomes invisible to low-vision users without programmatic fallback.\n- **No skip link or landmark structure context**: While this is an isolated component, it lacks surrounding `<main>`/`<nav>` context. In a page layout, keyboard users would tab through repeated instances without bypass mechanisms.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No role/expanded state; decorative symbols announced; no controls association |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | Unreachable trigger (no tabIndex/focusable element); no Enter/Space handlers |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Focus indicator missing in code; text contrast unknown but likely passable if styled |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Interaction model unclear (looks like text, not a button); state change visual-only |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations/transitions present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | No color-dependent state indicators beyond `+`/`-` symbols |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component is REJECTED because it fails at the foundational layer of accessibility design. It does not implement a WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern; it implements a mouse-only toggle with zero assistive technology support. Keyboard users cannot interact with the widget at all, and screen reader users receive no context about interactivity, state, or relationships. These are not minor gaps or stylistic preferences\u2014they are complete access barriers. The fix requires replacing `<div>` with `<button>`, adding `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, implementing standard keyboard handlers, and hiding decorative symbols. Once these core patterns are implemented, the component can be re-reviewed for compliance and design coherence.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- What is the surrounding page structure? If this component appears in a list or form, focus management between instances may need scoping.\n- Are there CSS styles attached to `.expandable-trigger` that add `cursor: pointer`, `tabIndex`, or `role=\"button\"` via a library (e.g., styled-components, Tailwind)? If so, those must be verified in the rendered DOM, not assumed from class names.\n- Does the `content` prop contain interactive elements (links, inputs)? If yes, focus should ideally move into the panel on expand per APG guidelines.",
